BeeZip: archives from the command line

Usage: beezip [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  run         Run any command by name, with JSON arguments
  commands    List every command
  schema      Print a command's input and output JSON schemas
  mcp         Serve BeeZip's tools over MCP on stdin/stdout
  version     Show the BeeZip version and build information.
  add         Add files and folders to an archive (zip, 7z, tar, cpio and ar, and tar and cpio under
              any codec). Each input is stored under its own name, as `archive.create` stores them,
              in `dir` if given; an ar holds files only. The archive isn't written in place: a new
              one is written beside it and swapped in, so a failed or cancelled add leaves it as it
              was. Entries that stay are copied without decoding. An archive whose entries are
              encrypted needs a password for the new files, unless `unencrypted`. [alias: a]
  convert     Write an archive's entries to a new archive in another format, or with other options:
              zip, 7z, tar, cpio, ar, cab, ISO, squashfs or DMG, tar and cpio under a codec too,
              from any archive BeeZip reads (RAR, packages, disk images and split archives
              included). The source isn't changed. Entries are copied without decoding when the
              formats match and no option asks for something else (`a.tar.gz` → `a.tar.zst` keeps
              every tar entry, encoding the stream again). An encrypted source needs `new_password`
              or `decrypt`: encryption is never dropped silently. What the new format can't hold is
              left out and reported in `skipped`; hard links become files where the format has none.
  create      Create an archive from files and folders. Each input is stored under its own name
              (`src/` becomes `src/…`). The format comes from `format` or the output's extension
              (`.zip`, `.7z`, `.tar`, `.tar.zst`, `.tgz`, `.cpio.gz`, `.ar`, `.cab`, `.iso`,
              `.squashfs`, `.dmg`…); ar and cab hold files only. An ISO has Joliet and Rock Ridge, a
              DMG is an HFS+ volume in a zlib-compressed image. The archive is written to a
              temporary file and renamed into place, so a failed or cancelled create leaves nothing
              behind. [alias: c]
  extract     Extract an archive, or the selected entries. Unsafe entries (paths climbing out with
              `..`, links leading out) are skipped and the rest extracted; the command then fails
              with `Unsafe` and the report in `details`. Without `dest`, files go into the current
              directory. [alias: x]
  info        Summarise an archive: its format chain, entry counts, sizes and compression ratio,
              methods, encryption, whether it is solid or self-extracting, and its comment.
  list        List an archive's entries. Without `flat`, one folder level at a time (the root, or
              `dir`), with folders that only exist as parts of paths shown too; with `flat` or a
              `glob`, every entry under `dir`. Results come in pages with `offset` and `limit`.
              [alias: l]
  remove      Remove entries from an archive (zip, 7z, tar, cpio and ar, and tar and cpio under any
              codec). A folder takes everything under it. A new archive is written beside the old
              one and swapped in, so a failed or cancelled removal leaves it as it was; the entries
              that stay are copied without decoding, except the rest of a 7z solid block that loses
              one, which is encoded again. Fails with `NotFound` when nothing matches. [alias: d]
  rename      Rename an entry of an archive, or a folder and everything under it (zip, 7z, tar, cpio
              and ar, and tar and cpio under any codec). `to` is the new full path, so a rename can
              also move an entry. The new name mustn't be taken, unless only its case differs.
              Entries are copied without decoding, encrypted ones included; renamed names are stored
              in UTF-8. A new archive is written beside the old one and swapped in. [alias: rn]
  test        Test an archive's integrity: read every entry to the end, checking CRCs and codec
              checksums. Fails with `Corrupt`, listing the damaged entries in `details`. [alias: t]
  open        Open an archive's file with its default app (Windows' "Open with" dialog when there is
              none). The entry is extracted, with the archive's Mark of the Web, into a folder of
              its own under `%TEMP%/BeeZip/open`, so two entries of one name don't collide; folders
              there older than a day are deleted as it goes. Programs run as they would from
              Explorer: SmartScreen sees the mark.
  read        Read part of one entry without extracting it, as text, hex or base64. An offset is
              reached by decoding up to it, since a compressed entry can't seek.
  detect      Detect what a file is: its chain of codecs and archive format (`zstd → tar`), whether
              it is a self-extracting archive, and what it is when it isn't an archive at all.
  formats     List the archive formats and codecs BeeZip reads and writes, with their capabilities,
              methods, levels and threading.
  compress    Compress one file with a single-stream codec (`a.txt` → `a.txt.zst`). The output keeps
              the input's modification time; the input is left in place.
  decompress  Decompress a compressed file, one codec layer per call: `a.tar.zst` → `a.tar`, `a.tgz`
              → `a.tar`, `notes.txt.gz` → `notes.txt`. The output keeps the input's modification
              time; the input is left in place.
  settings    Show and change BeeZip's settings (config.toml)
  shell       BeeZip's Explorer menu: what a click on it does
  help        Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Global options:
      --output <FORMAT>
          How to print results; ndjson streams every job event

          Possible values:
          - human:  Text for people
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout: the output, or `{"error": {...}}`
          - ndjson: Every job event as a JSON line on stdout, ending with `Finished`, `Failed` or
            `Cancelled`
          
          [default: human]

  -q, --quiet
          No progress bars or info messages; warnings still print
